430:
(a) Employer means any individual, partnership, association, corporation or any person or group of persons acting directly or indirectly on behalf of, and shall also include any public or privately owned corporation, all branches of the City of East Point, or any authority, commission or board or any other agency or instrumentality thereof. Employer shall also include agents, contractors or subcontractors of an employer.
(b) Employee means any individual who performs services for or under the control and direction of an employer for wages or other remuneration. Employee shall also include applicants for employment, former employees or an authorized representative of an employee.
(c) Public body means:
(1) The popularly elected local governmental body, or any member or employee thereof;
(2) Any local judiciary, or any member or employee thereof, or any grand or petit jury;
(3) Any local regulatory, administrative, or public agency or authority, or instrumentality thereof;
(4) Any local law enforcement agency, prosecutorial office, or police or peace officer;
(5) Any local department of an executive branch of government; or
(6) Any division, board, bureau, office, committee of any of the public bodies described in the above paragraphs of this subsection.
(d) Supervisor means any individual with an employer's organization who has the authority to direct and control the work performance of the affected employee or who has authority to take corrective action regarding the violation of the law, rule or regulation of which the employee complains.
(e) Retaliatory action means the discharge, suspension, demotion, harassment, blacklisting or the refusal to hire an employee, or other adverse employment, or other actions which interfere with an employee ability to engage in protected activity set forth in Code Section 4-1163 of this chapter.
(f) Improper quality of patient care means, with respect to patient care by an employer that is a health care provider, any practice, procedure, action or failure to act which violates any law or any rule, regulation or declaratory ruling adopted pursuant to law, or any professional code of ethics.
(Ord. No. 002-03, § 1, 1-21-03)

431:
An employer shall not take any retaliatory action against an employee because the employee does any of the following:
(a) Discloses, threatens to disclose or is about to disclose to a supervisor or to a public body, an activity, policy or practice of the employer, a co-employee or another employer, that the employee reasonably believes is in violation of a law, or a rule or regulation promulgated pursuant to law, or, in the case of an employee who is licensed or certified health care professional, reasonably believes constitutes improper quality of patient care;
(b) Provides information to, or testifies before, any public body conducting an investigation, hearing or inquiry into any violation of law, or a rule or regulation promulgated pursuant to law by the employer or another employer, or, in the case of an employee who is a licensed or certified health care professional, provides information to, or testifies before, any public body conducting an investigation, hearing or inquiry into the quality of patient care;
(c) Discloses, threatens to disclose or is about to disclose to a supervisor or to a public body, an activity, policy or practice of the employer, a co-employee or another employer, that the employee reasonably believes is incompatible with a clear mandate of public policy concerning the public health, safety or welfare or protection of the environment;
(d) Assists, or participates in a proceeding to enforce the provisions of the law; or
(e) Objects to, opposes or refuses to participate in any activity, policy or practice which the employee reasonably believes:
(1) Is in violation of a law, or a rule or regulation promulgated pursuant to law or, if the employee is a licensed or certified health care professional, constitutes improper quality of patient care;
(2) Is fraudulent or criminal; or
(3) Is incompatible with a clear mandate of public policy concerning public health, safety or welfare or protection of the environment.
(Ord. No. 002-03, § 1, 1-21-03)

434:
All remedies available in common law tort actions shall be available to prevailing plaintiffs. The court shall also, where appropriate, order:
(a) An injunction to restrain continued violation of this act;
(b) The reinstatement of the employee to the same position held before the retaliatory action, or to an equivalent position;
(c) The reinstatement of fill fringe benefits and seniority rights;
(d) The compensation for lost wages, benefits and other remuneration;
(e) The payment by the employer of reasonable costs, expert witness and attorney's fees; and
(f) Compensatory or exemplary damages.
(Ord. No. 002-03, § 1, 1-21-03)

452:
(a) The city may refuse to provide for the defense of a claim or proceeding brought against any employee if it determines that any one or more of the following conditions exists:
(1) The act or omission forming the basis of the claim or proceeding did not arise in the course and scope of the employees duties for the city;
(2) The employee acted or failed to act because of actual or intentional misconduct, fraud, corruption, malice, or bad faith;
(3) The claim or proceeding is criminal in nature or the employee acted or failed to act in such manner as to constitute a criminal act;
(4) The employee acted or failed to act as a result of, or at a time when, the employee's self-indulgence substantially impaired the employee's judgment, including, without limitation, an employee being intoxicated or under the influence of illegal narcotics while acting on behalf of the city;
(5) The employee acted or failed to act, except in under extenuating circumstances, directly contrary to advice of the city attorney or safety personnel;
(6) The employee fails to cooperate in the defense of the claim or proceeding or knowingly supplies false information regarding the claim or proceeding;
(7) The employee acted or failed to act in willful disregard of city policy or the specific instructions of the city council or the employee's supervisor; or
(8) Provision of a defense against the claim or proceeding would not be in the best interest of the city.
(b) The determinations required by subsection (a) of this section shall be made solely by the city council upon the advice of the city attorney and upon due consideration of any internal investigation the council may require. The city attorney may commence an initial representation and defense of an employee if the claim or proceeding requires that action be taken before the next scheduled meeting of council and the city attorney concludes, in his or her discretion, that the claim or proceeding would satisfy the criteria for defense by the city. At any time prior to or after the approval or assumption of the defense, the city may discontinue or refuse to provide the defense of an employee if the council determines that any of the grounds for refusal set forth in this section exists or has come to exist.
(c) Upon approval by city council of the defense of a claim or proceeding against an employee, the matter shall be referred to the city attorney or an outside attorney for disposition.
(d) Nothing in this article shall be construed to prohibit the city from providing a defense against claims or proceedings where there is a conflict between one employee or group of employees as compared with another employee or group of employees. If two or more employees or groups of employees are involved in a claim or proceeding, and the interest of one employee or group of employees conflicts with the interest of another employee or group of employees, city council shall approve or refuse to provide a defense to the respective employees or groups of employees in accordance the terms of subsection (a).
(e) If the city approves providing a defense to two (2) or more employees or groups of employees involved in a claim or proceeding, and the interest of one employee or group of employees conflicts with the interest of another employee or group of employees, council shall specify which employee or group of employees shall be represented by the city attorney and shall authorize the other employee or group of employees to retain separate counsel at the expense of the city; provided, however, that the selection and compensation of such separate counsel shall be subject to the prior approval of the city council.
(Ord. No. 009-05, § 4, 5-16-05)

499:
Unless otherwise authorized by law, all city contracts in an amount greater than twelve thousand five dollars ($12,500.00), except for the purchase, sale and disposition of real estate, shall be awarded by competitive sealed bidding, pursuant to section 4-3302 (Competitive sealed bidding), except as provided in:
(1) Section 4-3303 (Competitive sealed proposals);
(2) Section 4-3304 (Small purchases);
(3) Section 4-3305 (Sole source procurement);
(4) Section 4-3306 (Emergency procurement);
(5) Section 4-3307 (Competitive selection procedures for professional and consultant services);
(6) Section 4-3704 (Recurring inventory items);
(7) Section 4-3707 (Disposition of surplus supplies).
(8) Section 4-3323 (Aggregated purchasing groups).
(Ord. No. 1111-95, § 1, 7-3-95; Ord. No. 034-015, 8-17-15)

505:
(1) Conditions for use: The purchasing agent, upon the request or recommendation of the department as provided in section 4-3205 (Requirements upon using departments), shall procure professional and consultant services in accordance with this section, except as authorized under section 4-3305 (Sole source procurement) and section 4-3306 (Emergency procurement).
(2) Statement of qualifications: In the procurement of professional and consultant services, the purchasing agent shall request persons to submit statements of interest in providing such types of services with their statement of qualifications and performance data for inclusion in a professional or consultant qualifications list that shall be maintained by the purchasing agent. Each using department shall encourage professional and consultant firms, which desire to provide services to the department, to submit statements of qualifications to the purchasing agent. The purchasing agent may specify a uniform format for statements of qualifications. Persons may amend these statements at any time by filing a new statement. Any person placed on the qualifications list shall be removed if found in violation of this article within this chapter or in default of any contract with the city.
(3) Public notice: Where the estimated cost is in excess of twenty-five thousand dollars ($25,000.00), the purchasing agent shall issue public notice for request for proposals from professional and consultant offerors as provided in section 4-3302(3) (Competitive sealed bidding, public notice). The public notice for request for proposals shall describe the services required, list the type of information and data required of each offeror, and state the relative importance of particular qualifications.
(4) Selection process: The purchasing agent, in consultation with the head of the department and upon the recommendation of the head of the department, shall conduct discussions regarding the contract and shall select from among the offerors no less than three (3) offerors deemed most qualified to provide the required services. Provided, that the purchasing agent may waive the requirement of three (3) offerors upon the written determination that three (3) firms are not available to perform the proposed professional or consultant services. The selection shall be made in order of preference, based upon criteria established and published by the purchasing agent with the recommendation of the department head. The criteria shall include but not be limited to the following factors:
(a) Previous experience demonstrating competence to perform work involved in the project;
(b) Past performance of previous contracts with respect to time of completion and quality of work;
(c) The fee or compensation demanded for the work or services;
(d) The ability to comply with the applicable ordinances and resolutions of the city, the regulations and ordinances of the county in which the work is to be performed, and the laws of the State of Georgia and the United States;
(e) The ability to comply with the schedule of the commencement and completion of the work or services as required by the city;
(f) The financial ability to furnish the necessary bonds;
(g) The financial condition of the professional or consultant; and
(h) The ability to provide staffing of management personnel to the project, satisfactory to the city.
(5) Negotiations and award: The purchasing agent shall negotiate a contract with the most qualified firm for the professional or consultant services to be performed at compensation which the purchasing agent determines in writing to be fair and reasonable to the city. In making this decision, the purchasing agent shall take into account the estimated value, the scope, the complexity and the professional nature of the services to be rendered. Should the purchasing agent be unable to negotiate a satisfactory contract with the firm considered to be the most qualified at a price the purchasing agent determines to be fair and reasonable to the city, negotiations with that firm shall be formally terminated. The purchasing agent shall then undertake negotiations with the second most qualified firm. Failing accord with the second most qualified firm, the purchasing agent shall undertake negotiations with the third most qualified firm. Should the purchasing agent be unable to negotiate a contract at a fair and reasonable price with any of the selected firms, the purchasing agent and the using department shall select additional firms in order of their competence and qualifications and the purchasing agent shall continue negotiations in accordance with this section until an agreement is reached. After reaching an agreement the proposed contract will be sent back to council for final approval prior to its execution by the purchasing agent and the mayor.
(Ord. No. 1111-95, § 1, 7-3-95; Ord. No. 011-03, § 1, 3-3-03)

510:
(1) General insurance requirements: The purchasing agent, in consultation with the city treasurer, shall, at least annually, determine the minimum insurance requirements of the city and evaluate current insurance policies to determine whether minimum insurance requirements continue to be met. The purchasing agent, with the approval of the city manager and in consultation with the city treasurer, shall develop procedures for the purchase of insurance for the city, in accordance with this ordinance. Insurance requirements for the city shall be considered for the following purposes:
(a) Bonds, covenants or conditions of indebtedness of the city;
(b) Any employee benefit program;
(c) Public liability insurance for contractors doing business with the city;
(d) Any other purpose for which insurance is required or expedient, as determined by the purchasing agent, after consultation with the city manager.
(2) Public liability insurance for contracts: The purchasing agent is authorized to establish standard ranges for limits of liability in public liability insurance coverage, as appropriate, for all contracts let by the city where work thereunder will expose the general public to the risks of bodily injury and property damage. Where the purchasing agent determines that it is appropriate to the type of work under the contract and maximizes competition in the bidding or proposal process, lower limits of liability may be prescribed.
(Ord. No. 1111-95, § 1, 7-3-95)

540:
All city contracts for equipment, supplies, services, construction, and professional and consultant services shall include provisions necessary to define the responsibilities and rights of the parties to the contract. The purchasing agent, after consultation with the city attorney, may issue clauses appropriate for equipment, supply, service, construction, and professional and consultant service contracts, addressing the following subjects:
(1) The unilateral right of the city to order in writing changes in work within the scope of the contract;
(2) The unilateral right of the city to order in writing temporary stopping of the work or delaying performance that does not alter the scope of the contract;
(3) Variations occurring between estimated quantities of work in a contract and actual quantities;
(4) Defective pricing;
(5) Liquidated damages;
(6) Specified excuses of delay or nonperformance;
(7) Termination of the contract for default;
(8) Termination of the contract in whole or in part for the convenience of the city;
(9) Suspension of work on a construction contract ordered by the city; and
(10) Site conditions differing from those indicated in the contract, or ordinarily encountered, except that differing site conditions clause need not be included in a contract:
(a) When the contract is negotiated;
(b) When the contractor provides the site or design; or
(c) When the parties have otherwise agreed with respect to the risk of differing site conditions.
(d) In the absence of agreement by the parties, by a unilateral determination by the city of the costs attributable to the events or situations under such clauses with adjustment of profit or fee as computed by the city, as accounted for in accordance with Generally Accepted Accounting Principles and subject to the provisions of 4-3801 (Appeals and remedies).
(11) A contractor shall be required to submit cost or pricing data if any adjustment in contract price is subject to the provisions of section 4-3314 (Cost or pricing data).
(Ord. No. 1111-95, § 1, 7-3-95)

590:
All claims for damage against the city shall be promptly referred to the city attorney for investigation and written recommendation. Payments of any such claim shall not be recommended, nor will the city pay, settle or compromise any claim until it has been reduced to judgment in a court of competent jurisdiction, except:
(1) when supported by clear and convincing proof;
(2) when there is a clear and definite legal liability;
(3) when it is required by equity and good morals;
(4) when the amount is clear and liquidated, or there is a formula or standard by which the exact amount can be definitely computed without litigation or speculation; and
(5) when the claim has been filed with the city clerk in due time on a claim form furnished by the city.
(Code 1959, § 2-52)

611:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this chapter, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
(a) Declaration of domestic partnership shall mean a form provided by the city business license office in which two (2) people agree to be jointly responsible for the necessities of life incurred during the domestic partnership and that all qualifications for domestic partnership are met when the declaration is signed. The form will require both partners to provide reasonable proof of their primary, regular and permanent residence address. The form must be signed under penalty of perjury and must be witnessed and notarized. The business license office shall, within thirty (30) days of the effective date of this ordinance, make such forms available. To help defray costs associated with the providing and filing of these forms, the business license office is authorized to charge a fee as set forth herein for each completed form.
(b) Domestic partnership shall mean two (2) adults who live together in the mutual interdependence of a single home and have signed a declaration of domestic partnership in which they attest that:
(1) They share the same primary, regular and permanent residence and have lived together for the previous twelve (12) months (documentation must be submitted verifying joint residency);
(2) They have a committed personal relationship with each other that is mutually interdependent and intended to be lifelong;
(3) They agree to be jointly obligated and responsible for the necessities of life for each other;
(4) They are not married to anyone or legally separated from anyone;
(5) They are eighteen (18) years of age or older;
(6) They are of the same gender;
(7) They are competent to enter into a contract;
(8) They are not related by blood closer than would bar marriage in the state;
(9) They are each other's sole domestic partner;
(10) They agree to file a termination of domestic partnership within thirty (30) days if any of the facts set out in this definition change; and
(11) Any prior domestic partnership in which their domestic partner participated with a third party was terminated not less than twelve (12) months prior to the date of such affidavit, and, if such earlier domestic partnership had been acknowledged under provisions of this section, that notice of termination of such earlier domestic partnership was provided to the city in writing to the business license division of the department of finance not less than twelve (12) months prior to the date of said affidavit. If the prior domestic partnership was terminated by the death of the partner, there shall be no waiting period.
(c) Live together shall mean that two (2) people claiming domestic partnership status share the same primary, regular and permanent residence. It is not necessary that the legal right to possess the residence be in both names. Domestic partners do not cease to live together if one leaves the shared residence for a period not to exceed one (1) year, but intends to return. Whether the relationship between these two people is or is not sexual is in no way relevant for the purposes of determining eligibility under this chapter.
(d) Necessities of life shall mean the cost of basic food, shelter, clothing and medical care. The individuals need not contribute equally or jointly to the cost of these expenses as long as they agree that both are responsible and obligated for the cost.
(Ord. No. 005-05, § 1, 4-4-05)
